# 2. Put it in your slides

The demo is now a web page. A slide shows it in a frame, an `<iframe>`, if the slide tool can show a web
page while presenting. HTML slides can, natively; most other tools cannot, or only through an add-in.
Checked in November 2026, with the sources below.

| Tool | A live web page in a slide | How |
|---|---|---|
| reveal.js, Quarto, Slidev, Marp, lhtml-site | yes | an `<iframe>`, or the syntax of the tool |
| PowerPoint | only through a third-party add-in | a paid add-in, HTTPS addresses only |
| Keynote, Google Slides | no | a video, or a link to the page |
| LibreOffice Impress | no, in practice | a video, or a link |
| Beamer (PDF) | no | a video, or a link |

If your slides are not in HTML, the simplest is often to put the one slide with the demo in a small HTML
deck, or to switch to the browser for the demo (see [the other tools](#powerpoint-keynote-google-slides)).

## HTML slides

All of them show the page of the demo in an `<iframe>`. Serve the slides and the demo from the same web
server (a folder `demo/` next to the slides): the frame can then be driven from the slide, and the keys
reach the slides (step 3).

**reveal.js.** A frame in a slide, loaded when its slide comes near and unloaded when it leaves (`data-src`,
see [Media](https://revealjs.com/media/)):

```html
<section>
  <h3>A ripple</h3>
  <iframe class="demo" id="surface" data-src="demo/index.html?ampl=0.2"
          style="width: 100%; height: 520px; border: 0"></iframe>
</section>
```

A demo can also fill the whole slide as its background (`data-background-iframe`, with
`data-background-interactive` to use the mouse in it: [Backgrounds](https://revealjs.com/backgrounds/)).
reveal.js puts all the slides in one page: give each frame an `id`, and name it on the buttons of the
slide (`data-demo="#surface"`, step 3). [The example talk in reveal.js](../../decks/reveal.js/index.html).

**Quarto** (format `revealjs`): a raw `<iframe>` in the slide, or the whole slide,
`## A ripple {background-iframe="demo/index.html" background-interactive="true"}`
([Quarto presentations](https://quarto.org/docs/presentations/revealjs/index.html)).

**Slidev:** the layouts `iframe` (the page fills the slide), `iframe-left` and `iframe-right` (the page on
one side, your text on the other), in the front matter of the slide
([layouts](https://sli.dev/builtin/layouts)). With the demo in the folder `public/demo/` of the slides:

```yaml
---
layout: iframe-right
url: /demo/index.html?ampl=0.2
---
```

**Marp:** `<iframe>` is not in the HTML that Marp keeps by default: allow HTML (`html: true`, or
`marp --html`), then write the frame in the slide
([configuration](https://github.com/marp-team/marp-core/blob/main/docs/configuration.md)).

**lhtml-site:** the macro `demo::` writes the frame and sizes it in the layout of the slide, and the PDF of
the talk can show an image instead ([the example talk](../../decks/lhtml-site/html/index.html), its
[PDF](../../decks/lhtml-site/slides.pdf)):

```
media::
demo::demo/index.html?ampl=0.2
::
```

<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-title">The frame takes the keyboard</div>

Once you click in a demo, the keyboard goes to the frame: the arrow keys and the remote control stop
changing the slide. This is how browsers work, in every HTML slide tool. The page of the demo of this
tutorial sends the keys back to the slides ([step 3](../03_drive/index.html#the-keys-of-the-slides)).

</div>

## PowerPoint, Keynote, Google Slides

**PowerPoint.** Microsoft retired its own *Web Viewer* add-in in December 2024
([MC921112](https://mc.merill.net/message/MC921112)); PowerPoint has no built-in web page object. A
third-party add-in, [Web Viewer 2.0](https://marketplace.microsoft.com/en-us/product/wa200010526) (paid,
by 1990quebec), shows a live page in a slide, on Windows, Mac and the web according to its listing, with
`https://` addresses. Not documented: whether it shows `http://localhost`, works offline, or runs WebGL and
WebAssembly. The add-ins of Office run in Edge WebView2 on Windows and in Safari WKWebView on Mac
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/office/dev/add-ins/concepts/browsers-used-by-office-web-add-ins)),
which both have WebGL 2 and WebAssembly: a demo served over HTTPS may work, to be tested on your machine
before the talk.

**Keynote** inserts web videos from YouTube and Vimeo only, and needs a connection to play them
([Keynote guide](https://support.apple.com/guide/keynote/tan63d61519a/mac)). **Google Slides** inserts
videos from YouTube and Google Drive ([help](https://support.google.com/docs/answer/97447?hl=en)). Neither
shows a live web page.

**LibreOffice Impress** has *Insert > Floating Frame*, but its content is reported not to be shown during the
slide show ([bug 76962](https://www.libreoffice.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=76962)).

**Beamer** makes a PDF: no live web page. Its packages embed videos; the 3D of
[media9](https://ctan.org/pkg/media9) needs Adobe Reader.

What works with all of them:

- **A link to the page**, on the slide (a button, a QR code for the audience): the demo opens in the browser,
  in full screen (F11), and you come back to the slides after. Serve it locally (`python3 -m http.server`).
- **A video of the demo**, played in the slide. The demo can record itself frame by frame, with a fixed time
  step, so that the video is smooth ([step 4](../04_talk-day/index.html#a-video-as-plan-b)).
- **One HTML slide** for the demo, in a small reveal.js deck that you open when you reach it.

